{% comment %}
  blocks/badge-highlight.liquid

  One trust badge: an icon, an optional pill, a heading and some text.

  ## The icon is the Feature icon's, control for control

  The glyph, the shape behind it and the ring around that are the same three
  groups `blocks/_feature-icon.liquid` offers, with the same setting ids, the
  same labels and the same meanings — Icon / Shape / Ring. A merchant who has
  styled a feature card already knows this panel, and a designer specifying one
  mark specifies both.

  What is deliberately *not* copied is the connectors. Those are the feature
  card's spine, tying a title and a paragraph into one column; a badge is a tile
  with its words beside the mark, and there is nothing above or below for a
  hairline to join.

  Two additions the shared vocabulary needed:

    Dotted ring   the badge's old frame offered it and tiles in the theme use
                  it. `stroke-dasharray` with round caps draws it, so it is one
                  more dash pattern rather than a second code path.

    Gradient fill **Fill color** is a `color_background`, because the design's
                  mark is a green gradient squircle. The Feature icon's is a
                  flat `color`; a gradient is a superset and nothing about a
                  flat colour changes.

  ### The old frame settings still render

  Before this the icon sat in `icon_frame_*` — a style, a size, a px width, a
  *percentage* radius, a colour and a background. Those ids are gone from the
  panel but every one of them is still read, as the fallback beneath the new
  setting, so a badge saved against the old vocabulary draws exactly the mark it
  drew before: the frame becomes a Shape with a Ring at zero offset, which is
  the same picture by a different name. The moment a merchant touches the new
  controls their value wins and the legacy read is over.

  ## The custom SVG is a source, not a race

  Custom SVG, an upload and the theme set used to be tried in that order, and a
  merchant discovered which had won by clearing fields until the right mark
  appeared. **Icon** now states the source and only its own field is shown —
  the same rule `blocks/subheading.liquid` keeps.

  Pasted SVG is not escaped — it cannot be, or the field is useless — and that is
  the merchant's own input from their own admin, the same trust boundary
  Shopify's Custom Liquid uses. It is wrapped in a span rather than inserted
  bare, so a paste missing its closing tag breaks one badge instead of the
  section below it.

  ## The link wraps nothing

  When a link is set, a stretched pseudo-element covers the tile rather than an
  anchor wrapping the content. Wrapping gives a screen reader one link named with
  every word in the badge, and swallows anything interactive added later.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  assign has_link = false
  if s.link != blank
    assign has_link = true
  endif

  comment
    ---- where the mark comes from ----

    Stated, not raced. `icon_source` is the new control; a badge saved before it
    existed has nothing in it, and for those the old order — pasted SVG, then an
    upload, then the theme set — is reproduced here so the mark they are showing
    is the mark they keep.
  endcomment
  assign icon_source = s.icon_source
  if icon_source == blank
    if s.custom_svg != blank
      assign icon_source = 'svg'
    elsif s.icon_image != blank
      assign icon_source = 'image'
    else
      assign icon_source = 'icon'
    endif
  endif

  comment
    The field belonging to the source has to actually hold something. A merchant
    who picks Image and has not uploaded one yet gets the words on their own
    rather than an empty box holding a gap open — the same rule
    `blocks/subheading.liquid` and `blocks/_feature-icon.liquid` both keep.
  endcomment
  assign has_icon = false
  if icon_source == 'icon' and s.icon != blank and s.icon != 'none'
    assign has_icon = true
  elsif icon_source == 'image' and s.icon_image != blank
    assign has_icon = true
  elsif icon_source == 'svg' and s.custom_svg != blank
    assign has_icon = true
  endif

  capture icon_px
    render 'icon-size', size: s.icon_size, custom: s.icon_size_custom
  endcapture
  assign icon_size = icon_px | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- the surface ----

    The alpha goes into the colour rather than onto the element, the same split
    `blocks/card-content.liquid` sets out at length: `opacity` here would fade
    the icon and the words along with the tile they are read against.

    Default 100, so a badge saved before the slider existed paints exactly the
    colour it was given.
  endcomment
  assign background_paint = ''
  if s.background != blank
    assign background_alpha = s.background_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    assign background_alpha = background_alpha | divided_by: 100.0
    assign background_paint = s.background | color_modify: 'alpha', background_alpha
  endif

  comment
    ---- the box ----

    A fixed width and height, because a row of these is a row of tiles and tiles
    that size themselves to their own sentence are a row of different boxes.
    Zero is the off switch and the value a badge saved before this existed
    carries, so those keep sizing to their content exactly as they did.

    `min-` rather than a hard size — see the note in `base.css`. A merchant who
    writes four lines into a 142px tile gets a taller tile rather than text
    spilling out of it.
  endcomment
  assign box_width = s.width | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign box_height = s.height | default: 0 | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- the frame the old vocabulary drew ----

    Read first, and only ever used as a fallback below. `icon_frame_style` was
    one control doing the work of two — a shape *and* a line around it — with a
    radius stated as a percentage of its own size. The new vocabulary splits
    those apart, so a saved frame is translated once, here, rather than tested
    for in six places further down.

    50% of a square box is a circle and anything under it is a rounded square:
    that is what the old radius meant, so it is what decides the Shape.
  endcomment
  assign frame_style = s.icon_frame_style | default: 'none'
  assign frame_size = s.icon_frame_size | default: 56 | plus: 0
  assign frame_radius_pct = s.icon_frame_radius | default: 50 | plus: 0

  assign frame_shape = 'none'
  if frame_style != 'none'
    if frame_radius_pct >= 50
      assign frame_shape = 'circle'
    else
      assign frame_shape = 'square'
    endif
  endif
  assign frame_radius_px = frame_size | times: frame_radius_pct | divided_by: 100

  comment
    ---- the shape behind the glyph ----

    `blocks/_feature-icon.liquid`'s three values and its meanings: None leaves
    the glyph on its own and hides the fill, the size and the radius with it,
    because there is no box for any of them to describe.

    No `default` in the schema, deliberately. A select with one resolves to its
    default the moment it is read, which cannot be told apart from a merchant
    choosing that value — and the difference is exactly what decides whether a
    badge saved against `icon_frame_style` keeps its frame. Nil means "never
    answered", so the old frame answers; anything else is the merchant's own.
  endcomment
  assign shape = s.shape
  if shape == blank
    assign shape = frame_shape
  endif

  assign shape_size = s.shape_size | default: frame_size | plus: 0
  assign shape_size_mobile = s.shape_size_mobile | default: shape_size | plus: 0

  comment
    A circle is a radius of half the box at every size, so it is derived rather
    than asked for — the Corner radius slider belongs to Square and is hidden
    on Circle for the same reason Fill is hidden on None.
  endcomment
  if shape == 'circle'
    assign shape_radius = shape_size
  else
    assign shape_radius = s.shape_radius | default: frame_radius_px | plus: 0
  endif

  comment
    ---- the ring ----

    Offset is the clear space between the shape and the ring, so the ring's own
    size is derived rather than asked for twice — the same arrangement, and the
    same reasoning, as the Feature icon's.

    It defaults to 0 here rather than to the feature card's 11. At zero the ring
    is drawn on the shape's own edge, which is a border — and a border is what
    the frame this replaces always drew, so a translated badge lands exactly
    where it was.
  endcomment
  assign ring_style = s.ring_style | default: frame_style
  assign has_ring = false
  if ring_style != 'none' and shape != 'none'
    assign has_ring = true
  endif

  assign ring_width = s.ring_width | default: s.icon_frame_width | default: 1 | plus: 0
  assign ring_offset = s.ring_offset | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign ring_size = shape_size | plus: ring_offset | plus: ring_offset
  assign ring_size_mobile = shape_size_mobile | plus: ring_offset | plus: ring_offset

  comment
    The stroke is centred on the path, so the radius is inset by half the width
    or the ring is clipped by its own viewBox on both sides.
  endcomment
  assign ring_half = ring_size | divided_by: 2.0
  assign ring_radius = ring_size | minus: ring_width | divided_by: 2.0
  assign ring_inset = ring_width | divided_by: 2.0
  assign ring_box = ring_size | minus: ring_width

  comment
    With no ring there is no clear space to hold open, so the shape's box is the
    shape; with no shape either, the box is the glyph. Both matter to the
    stylesheet, which sizes the outer box from one property whatever is in it.
  endcomment
  unless has_ring
    assign ring_size = shape_size
    assign ring_size_mobile = shape_size_mobile
  endunless
  if shape == 'none'
    assign ring_size = icon_size
    assign ring_size_mobile = icon_size
  endif

  comment
    One code path for all three styles, so switching between them cannot change
    anything but the dash. Solid states no pattern at all. Dotted is a dash of
    no length with a round cap, which a browser draws as a circle the width of
    the stroke — the only way to get a true dot out of `stroke-dasharray`, and
    the reason the gap is stated relative to the width rather than asked for.
  endcomment
  assign ring_dash = ''
  assign ring_cap = 'butt'
  if ring_style == 'dashed'
    assign dash_on = s.ring_dash | default: 2 | plus: 0
    assign dash_off = s.ring_dash_gap | default: 8 | plus: 0
    assign ring_dash = dash_on | append: ' ' | append: dash_off
  elsif ring_style == 'dotted'
    comment
      The pitch is derived from the thickness rather than asked for, which is
      what dotted means everywhere else: CSS gives no control over a dotted
      border's spacing either, because a dot and the space beside it are one
      proportion and not two numbers. Twice the width is the spacing a browser
      draws, so a frame translated from the old vocabulary keeps the rhythm it
      had. Dash gap stays hidden here for the same reason.
    endcomment
    assign dash_off = ring_width | times: 2
    assign ring_dash = '0.01 ' | append: dash_off
    assign ring_cap = 'round'
  endif

  assign ring_spin = s.ring_spin | default: 0 | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- the colours ----

    Each falls back to the frame value it replaces, and every one of them is
    emitted only when it holds something — so a badge that never picked a fill
    still gets `transparent` from the stylesheet rather than a colour nobody
    chose.
  endcomment
  assign fill_paint = s.fill_color | default: s.icon_frame_background
  assign fill_hover_paint = s.fill_hover_color
  assign ring_paint = s.ring_color | default: s.icon_frame_color
  assign ring_hover_paint = s.ring_hover_color

  comment
    Frosted, for a tile read against a photograph. Same property, same rule
    shape and same caveat as `blocks/card-content.liquid`: `backdrop-filter`
    samples what is painted underneath, so over a flat colour it has nothing to
    frost and appears to do nothing. Zero by default, so no tile that existed
    before this gains a filter.
  endcomment
  assign backdrop_blur = s.backdrop_blur | default: 0 | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- a border that is a gradient ----

    **Border color** is a `color_background`, so the merchant can hand it either
    a flat colour or a gradient — the same field type the Group block's surface
    already uses, and the only one in Shopify that returns a gradient at all.

    A gradient cannot go through `border-color`: that property takes a colour,
    and a `linear-gradient(...)` handed to it is an invalid declaration the
    browser drops, which renders as no border at all rather than as an error the
    merchant can see. So the two cases take two different routes — a real border
    for a colour, a masked ring painted in `::before` for a gradient — and this
    is the test that decides which.

    `contains 'gradient'` rather than a parse: every gradient CSS can express
    has the word in its own name — `linear-gradient`, `radial-gradient`,
    `conic-gradient` and the `repeating-` forms — and no notation for a flat
    colour contains it.
  endcomment
  assign border_is_gradient = false
  if s.border_color contains 'gradient'
    assign border_is_gradient = true
  endif

  assign classes = 'badge-highlight badge-highlight--' | append: s.alignment
  if s.hover != 'none'
    assign classes = classes | append: ' badge-highlight--hover-' | append: s.hover
  endif
  if shape != 'none'
    assign classes = classes | append: ' badge-highlight--shape-' | append: shape
  endif
  if border_is_gradient
    assign classes = classes | append: ' badge-highlight--gradient-border'
  endif

  comment
    The colour scheme is the parent's unless the merchant says otherwise — the
    same control, spelled the same way, as `blocks/group.liquid`.

    It defaults the other way round from the Group block's, on purpose. A group
    is a container and often wants a surface of its own; a badge is a tile that
    sits inside one, and painting it a second scheme puts a rectangle of colour
    behind every item in a row that already has a background.

    That default is also what keeps this setting from changing anything that
    existed before it. This block had no scheme control at all and never painted
    one, so `false` here would have given every badge already in a theme a
    background it had never had — the section's scheme resolved and painted
    behind each tile. Inheriting is the behaviour they have; the picker is the
    new thing, and it is opt-in.

    `nil` rather than `blank` for the unset test: `false == blank` is true in
    Liquid, so a blank check would read a merchant's deliberate "off" as "not
    answered" and silently turn inheriting back on.
  endcomment
  assign inherit_scheme = s.inherit_color_scheme
  if inherit_scheme == nil
    assign inherit_scheme = true
  endif
  if inherit_scheme == false and s.color_scheme != blank
    assign classes = classes | append: ' color-scheme-container color-' | append: s.color_scheme
  endif
-%}

{%- capture badge_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  {%- if background_paint != '' -%}--badge-background: {{ background_paint }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.border_color != blank -%}
    {%- if border_is_gradient -%}
      --badge-border-paint: {{ s.border_color }};
    {%- else -%}
      --badge-border-color: {{ s.border_color }};
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    ---- which way the words run ----

    Separate from **Icon position**, because they answer different questions.
    Icon position lays the tile out — glyph above the words, beside them, or the
    pair centred as a column — and until now the text alignment came along with
    it: the row arrangements said nothing, so the words took whatever the
    container was doing. A tile in a right-aligned group came out right-aligned
    with its icon on the left, which is a tile nobody chose and could not fix
    from its own settings.

    `inherit` is the default and emits nothing at all, so every tile already in
    a theme keeps the alignment it has — including the centred one, whose
    `text-align: center` is part of the Icon position: Center layout and is not
    this setting's to undo.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if s.text_alignment != blank and s.text_alignment != 'inherit' -%}
    --badge-text-align: {{ s.text_alignment }};
  {%- endif -%}
  --badge-border-width: {{ s.border_width }}px;
  --badge-radius: {{ s.radius }}px;
  --badge-icon-size: {{ icon_size }}px;
  {%- if s.icon_color != blank -%}--badge-icon-color: {{ s.icon_color }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- if box_width > 0 -%}--badge-width: {{ box_width }}px;{%- endif -%}
  {%- if box_height > 0 -%}--badge-height: {{ box_height }}px;{%- endif -%}
  {%- if backdrop_blur > 0 -%}--badge-backdrop-blur: {{ backdrop_blur }}px;{%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Two names per size, one resolved by `base.css` at the breakpoint. Writing
    the mobile value into the same property is not an option:
    `--x: var(--x-mobile, var(--x))` is a self-reference, which resolves to the
    guaranteed-invalid value and takes the declaration down with it. Same pair,
    same reason, as the Feature icon's.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --badge-ring-size-desktop: {{ ring_size }}px;
  --badge-ring-size-mobile: {{ ring_size_mobile }}px;
  {%- if s.icon_hover_color != blank -%}--badge-icon-color-hover: {{ s.icon_hover_color }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- if shape != 'none' -%}
    --badge-shape-size-desktop: {{ shape_size }}px;
    --badge-shape-size-mobile: {{ shape_size_mobile }}px;
    --badge-shape-radius: {{ shape_radius }}px;
    {%- if fill_paint != blank -%}--badge-fill: {{ fill_paint }};{%- endif -%}
    {%- if fill_hover_paint != blank -%}--badge-fill-hover: {{ fill_hover_paint }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if has_ring -%}
    {%- if ring_paint != blank -%}--badge-ring-color: {{ ring_paint }};{%- endif -%}
    {%- if ring_hover_paint != blank -%}--badge-ring-color-hover: {{ ring_hover_paint }};{%- endif -%}
    {%- if ring_spin != 0 -%}--badge-ring-spin: {{ ring_spin }}deg;{%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

<div
  class="{{ classes }}"
  data-block-spacing
  style="{{ badge_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- comment -%}
    The box is only rendered when there is a mark to put in it. It carries a
    size of its own, so an empty one is a hole in the row holding a gap open
    beside words that have nothing to sit next to.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if has_icon or shape != 'none' -%}
    <div class="badge-highlight__icon">
      {%- if has_ring -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Drawn at the desktop size with a matching viewBox, so at that width the
          dash is exactly the length the merchant asked for. The stylesheet
          stretches it to whatever box it is in, so at the mobile size the dash
          scales with the ring rather than the desktop dash being wrapped around
          a smaller circle.

          A `border` cannot do this. Every browser picks its own dash length and
          gap from the width, so "2px dash, 8px gap" is not something CSS can be
          asked for — and Dotted needs a round cap on a zero-length dash, which
          a border has no way to express at all.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <svg
          class="badge-highlight__ring"
          width="{{ ring_size }}"
          height="{{ ring_size }}"
          viewBox="0 0 {{ ring_size }} {{ ring_size }}"
          fill="none"
          aria-hidden="true"
          focusable="false"
        >
          {%- if shape == 'square' -%}
            <rect
              x="{{ ring_inset }}"
              y="{{ ring_inset }}"
              width="{{ ring_box }}"
              height="{{ ring_box }}"
              rx="{{ shape_radius | plus: ring_offset }}"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="{{ ring_width }}"
              stroke-linecap="{{ ring_cap }}"
              {% if ring_dash != blank %}stroke-dasharray="{{ ring_dash }}"{% endif %}
            />
          {%- else -%}
            <circle
              cx="{{ ring_half }}"
              cy="{{ ring_half }}"
              r="{{ ring_radius }}"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="{{ ring_width }}"
              stroke-linecap="{{ ring_cap }}"
              {% if ring_dash != blank %}stroke-dasharray="{{ ring_dash }}"{% endif %}
            />
          {%- endif -%}
        </svg>
      {%- endif -%}

      <span class="badge-highlight__disc">
        {%- if icon_source == 'svg' and s.custom_svg != blank -%}
          <span class="badge-highlight__svg" aria-hidden="true">{{ s.custom_svg }}</span>
        {%- elsif icon_source == 'image' and s.icon_image != blank -%}
          <img
            class="badge-highlight__image"
            src="{{ s.icon_image | image_url: width: 160 }}"
            alt="{{ s.icon_image_alt | default: '' | escape }}"
            width="{{ icon_size }}"
            height="{{ icon_size }}"
            loading="lazy"
          >
        {%- elsif has_icon -%}
          {% render 'icon', icon: s.icon, size: icon_size, class: 'badge-highlight__glyph' %}
        {%- endif -%}
      </span>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

  <div class="badge-highlight__body">
    {%- if s.pill != blank -%}
      <span
        class="badge-highlight__pill"
        style="
          {%- if s.pill_background != blank %}--pill-background: {{ s.pill_background }};{% endif -%}
          {%- if s.pill_color != blank %}--pill-color: {{ s.pill_color }};{% endif -%}
        "
      >
        {{ s.pill }}
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if s.heading != blank -%}
      <h3 class="badge-highlight__heading">
        {%- if has_link -%}
          <a class="badge-highlight__link" href="{{ s.link }}">{{ s.heading }}</a>
        {%- else -%}
          {{ s.heading }}
        {%- endif -%}
      </h3>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if s.text != blank -%}
      <div class="badge-highlight__text rte">{{ s.text }}</div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.badge_highlight.name",
  "tag": null,
  "settings": [
    { "type": "header", "content": "t:blocks.badge_highlight.headers.icon" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "t:blocks.badge_highlight.paragraph" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "icon_source",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "none", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "icon", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "image", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.options__3.label" },
        { "value": "svg", "label": "t:blocks.badge_highlight.settings.icon_source.options__4.label" }
      ],
      "default": "icon"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "icon",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "star", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "check", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "heart", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__3.label" },
        { "value": "tag", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__4.label" },
        { "value": "truck", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__5.label" },
        { "value": "refresh", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__6.label" },
        { "value": "lock", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__7.label" },
        { "value": "clock", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__8.label" },
        { "value": "location", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__9.label" },
        { "value": "mail", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__10.label" },
        { "value": "note", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__11.label" },
        { "value": "info", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__12.label" },
        { "value": "chat", "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.icon.options__13.label" },
        { "value": "phone", "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.icon.options__14.label" },
        { "value": "share", "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.icon.options__15.label" },
        { "value": "grid", "label": "t:blocks.feature_icon.settings.icon.options__16.label" },
        { "value": "shield", "label": "t:blocks.icon_with_text.settings.icon.options__2.label" }
      ],
      "default": "truck",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'icon' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "icon_image",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_image.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_image.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'image' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "icon_image_alt",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_image_alt.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_image_alt.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'image' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "html",
      "id": "custom_svg",
      "label": "t:blocks.badge_highlight.settings.custom_svg.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.badge_highlight.settings.custom_svg.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.icon_source == 'svg' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "icon_size",
      "label": "t:shared.icon_size.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "extra_small", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "small", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "medium", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__3.label" },
        { "value": "large", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__4.label" },
        { "value": "extra_large", "label": "t:shared.icon_size.options__5.label" },
